== Outlook

#chap-quote("We can only see a short distance ahead, but we can see plenty there that needs to be done.",attribution: "Alan Turing")

Feynman predicted that computers might become quantum by 2050~@feynman_computation. Though he could not predict the difficulties we would meet along the way, it seems that his idea of quantum mechanical computers is slowly turning out to be true, as our NISQ computers turn fault-tolerant, and the algorithms that yesterday were beyond our grasp are run on today's devices. Looking forward, we hope that quantum digital cooling for fermionic systems beyond toy models becomes attainable, if not commonplace, on tomorrow's devices.

On a more philosophical note, it is startling to see how much of quantum mechanics is still a mystery, and the disagreements on what it tells us about reality are witnesses to its enduring strangeness. Quantum computing helps us lift the veil of this unknown, using a mystery to solve itself; and still, it matters little how advanced quantum devices get: our eyes only see the classical world, and only through it do we get closer to this stranger truth. On Helgoland, Anton Zeilinger said that quantum mechanics links classical data with classical data. To reformulate this in Feynman's words, "nature _is_ classical, dammit!"